3. Top five deep dives TinyRouter =E2=80=94 closest unseen JEV neighbour

This is structurally more important than another generic agent framework. A
frozen encoder maps a request into a compact representation and a
~10K-parameter head chooses a bounded action: model + role. That is
precisely the architectural split Privately has been looking for: expensive
generative cognition can propose state, while a cheap learned head selects
among bounded authorized options.

*Privately experiment:* create 8 bounded security decisions (ALLOW, DENY,
ASK_HUMAN, SANITIZE, QUARANTINE_MEMORY, CHEAP_MODEL, STRONG_MODEL, NO_TOOL)
and compare TinyRouter/JEV-like heads against an LLM judge under
latency/cost/adversarial perturbation. Falsifier: if the tiny head loses
materially on out-of-distribution authority decisions and cannot be
calibrated, keep it for non-security routing only.
Attestral =E2=80=94 whole-system agent attack graph

Most MCP scanners inspect one descriptor/config at a time. Attestral=E2=80=
=99s
interesting mechanism is graph composition: tools + prompts + framework
code + Terraform + Kubernetes + credential reach become one attack-surface
model. That aligns directly with Privately=E2=80=99s thesis that the danger=
ous
object is the authority path, not an isolated prompt.

*Privately experiment:* synthesize a benign three-tool environment where no
individual tool is dangerous but read_secret -> transform -> external_send
is. Compare Attestral, mcpgw, MCPCustoms and QP on whether they identify
the composed path.
